Jesuits explicitly took up images as a part of their programme of conversion of the peoples of Asia and the Americas and of reconversion of the peoples of Europe.[^p391-38]  They drew upon images to translate central Christian theological ideas to non-European cultures.[^p391-39]  They built new churches in Asia and the Americas, in the process connecting European visual traditions to local, at once preserving and transforming both.[^p391-40]  In the recatholicization of Bavaria, they commissioned new images for churches that had been ‘cleansed’, and built new churches, which were also visually rich (Fig. 8).[^p391-41]  In each milieu, the Jesuits both carried with them and commissioned from local artisans images that might bring to Europeans and to cultures whose languages the Jesuits had not yet mastered central theological concepts of Catholicism. When Pierre Biard arrived in 1610 with the first Jesuits at Acadia in Canada, for instance, ‘I took the occasion to give them images and to place a cross before their cabin, singing a Salve Regina’.[^p391-42]  The ‘neophytes’ among the Algonquins in the mid-seventeenth century turned their eyes to an image of the Virgin in times of suffering.[^p391-43]  So, too, the Jesuits foremost among the missionizing orders took up gold, its particular physical properties, to convey in material language qualities of God – no material captured quite so dramatically the ‘glory of God’.

In a number of devotional works, Jesuits suggested how they understood images to function in the processes of conversion and of deepening piety. Jerome Nadal commissioned a series of extraordinary images whose function was not to illustrate the text, but to focus the mind of the reader, to reach the soul through the eyes, not to ‘aid meditation’, but to bring the entire body into the act of meditation – eyes to heart.[^p391-44]  Jesuit art rested on the conviction that images might shape the perception of the world itself, through exercises;
